  "evidence": "A: Monthly Rwanda removals; rewrite asylum treaties; a legal annual cap on work/family migration that falls each year; higher visa fees and health checks; 8,000 more neighbourhood police, hotspot policing and facial recognition; tougher sentences including more whole-life terms; four new prisons; clarify in law that sex means biological sex and protect single-sex spaces; ban mobiles in school and strengthen parental rights on RSHE; mandatory National Service at 18; regulatory reform using Brexit freedoms; defence spending to 2.5% of GDP; strengthen the Union and internal market; Backing Drivers Bill (ban local road pricing, reverse London ULEZ expansion). B: A Race Equality Act and pay-gap reporting; a full ban on conversion practices; modernised gender recognition law; votes at 16; uphold the ECHR; zero‑carbon electricity by 2030, no new oil and gas licences, and Great British Energy; nature restoration and tougher water regulation; New Deal for Working People expanding worker rights.",
  "reasoning": "A argues from TAN commitments—restrictive immigration and asylum, tougher policing and sentencing, national service, traditional gender definitions, Brexit-sovereignty framing, and prioritising order—matching a TAN‑leaning electorate. B argues from GAL commitments—expanding minority and LGBTQ+ rights, franchise extension, and ambitious environmentalism—aligning less with the profile’s TAN=0.62, GAL=0.38 balance.",
